WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:00.934
OpenClaw is a hit,

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.302
and I've set it up so I can send it a message,

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.703
and it will create a podcast for me

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:04.871
and publish it to the internet.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:07.340
For example, I can ask it to summarize today's AI news,

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.808
Tesla's earnings report,

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:11.211
or even turn the first two chapters of a book into a podcast,

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:15.615
or create an audio tutorial from an article.

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.717
About ten minutes later, I can open my Apple Podcasts,

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.353
and an episode made just for me is already there.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:23.857
This podcast doesn't need platform approval to be published.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.226
It can be a private feed that only I can listen to,

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:30.030
or I can let my friends and community subscribe to it, and it's free.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.598
So, how is this possible?

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:35.235
Well, to make this happen, OpenClaw alone isn't enough.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:38.738
It also needs a private podcast platform that I "Vibe Coded" myself

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:40.774
called Dear Tape.

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:43.476
Right, it's like I went to a whole lot of trouble for something very specific.

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:46.946
So in this video, I want to share with you the private podcast platform I made,

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:49.482
Dear Tape,

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:52.952
and how to use it with OpenClaw to completely automate

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.721
podcast creation and publishing. First off, why did I even make

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.793
Dear Tape?

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:04.063
What is it, exactly? Well, it all started with a need I've had for a long time.

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:07.400
For a while now, I've wanted a private podcast.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.968
So, what's a private podcast?

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:13.406
It's... well, I want to create content, but only share it with specific people,

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:16.876
like my family, friends, or our community members.

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.778
But starting a podcast is actually quite a hassle.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.248
You have to submit it to Spotify or Apple, and

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.383
sometimes there's an approval process,

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.317
and all sorts of settings to configure.

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:28.421
The main problem is, once it's live, it has to be public. Anyone can search for it,

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.490
which defeats my goal of private sharing.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.294
Many years ago, I tried a website called HuffDuffer.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.764
The concept was simple: if you had a Dropbox link,

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.467
you could create your own podcast.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:43.937
But the problem was that all your content was public. Anyone could

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:45.038
subscribe to your podcast,

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:46.940
so I stopped using it.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.877
But it showed me that it was possible to use storage like Dropbox

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.946
to host my own podcast for free.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:56.416
Of course, back then, with no programming background, I never thought

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:57.684
I would ever build one myself.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:02.121
But then AI came along, and an opportunity arose that gave me the idea. Last summer,

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:05.592
my in-laws came to Canada to visit us, and I often drove them

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:07.026
out to the Rocky Mountains.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:11.297
Sometimes we'd be on the road for hours, and I'd think, "Let's listen to something."

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:14.934
Around that time, I had been using NotebookLM

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.503
to create some audiobook-style podcasts,

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.271
so I just played them.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:21.107
It turned out they loved them and started asking me for new episodes every day.

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:24.577
That's when I thought, if only I had a private podcast. My

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.479
content could be pushed directly to it,

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:30.283
and they could subscribe with the native podcast app on their iPhones to get updates.

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:32.485
No more hassle of sending files or links.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:36.456
Plus, it would be private between us, no one else could see it. This tool

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:40.560
could also let me create internal podcasts for our community. So, I decided to just do it.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.030
And Dear Tape was born. OK, this is great. This, this

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.298
is really awesome!

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.768
I was really bored after dinner, so I thought I'd try to see if my private

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.203
podcast idea could work.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:53.673
And what's crazy is that in just a few minutes, I had already set up

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.742
my own private podcast.

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.944
You can all have a listen. Yay!

00:02:57.944 --> 00:03:01.748
The first version was usable right away. It basically took two shots

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.016
to solve the problem.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:06.486
Oh, let me tell you, this Vibe Coding thing lately,

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:07.921
it's been keeping me up at night.

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:10.757
I can't even eat properly. All my bandwidth is consumed by it.

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:14.260
Look here, this is the project I most want to do.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.730
I believe... I believe it might be too early to say, but I believe it should go

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.567
pretty smoothly. But I spoke too soon.

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:24.370
Because I quickly ran into a major problem. Since I was inspired by, uh,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.272
HuffDuffer,

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:30.543
which uses Dropbox, I used Dropbox too.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:34.013
But I discovered that Dropbox will ban links

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.448
that are played too many times.

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:39.886
The link would become invalid after just a few plays, which meant the service would be very unstable.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:43.656
Of course, I could rent my own server and have people upload audio there,

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:46.993
but that would be very expensive, and it wouldn't be free anymore.

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:50.463
My idea was simple: users shouldn't have to pay extra. They just need

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:52.532
a free cloud storage account.

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.901
So I had to put the project on hold.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:59.005
But after a while, one day it suddenly occurred to me,

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:00.106
what about trying Google Drive?

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:03.610
I gave it a try, and it actually worked. And Google Drive's

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.011
limits are very generous;

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.279
you basically don't get banned.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:10.383
Looking back, it's actually pretty funny. It was like I had this mental block,

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:13.886
thinking that only Dropbox could do this,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:17.357
and no other free cloud storage would work. But this also proves that

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:17.690
when you hit a dead end,

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.628
if you just set it aside for a bit and then look at the problem again, you'll often find a breakthrough.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:25.431
So the project was revived. (Podcast audio) With the help of AI,

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:29.869
I spent a few weeks overcoming various challenges, like user logins, databases,

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.339
privacy protection, and my weakest skill, UI design, and finally,

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:34.741
I managed to build the platform.

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:39.012
I named it Dear Tape, and its slogan

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:41.080
is "Sound for the ones who care."

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.550
The problem it solves is actually very simple. First, it's free.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.354
You just need a free Google Drive account,

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:51.524
and you can use it. (Podcast audio) Second, there are no barriers to entry.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:55.962
You don't need to go through Apple's review process or submit to Spotify.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:05:00.233
Just paste in the audio link, and it will automatically generate a podcast subscription link.

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:03.703
Third, it's private. Only people with the subscription link can subscribe. Others

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.772
can't find it by searching in podcast apps.

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:09.242
Of course, you can also create, uh, multiple podcast channels, separated by

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.444
your interests or target audience.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:14.947
Fourth, no ecosystem lock-in. I think this is very important. Most

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:17.784
subscribers don't need to change their podcast listening habits.

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:22.055
You can use Apple Podcasts and most other clients

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:25.358
that support RSS subscriptions to listen to your station.

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:27.126
So what can you use it for?

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:30.897
Uh, for example, a teacher or coach could create an exclusive course podcast for their students,

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:34.400
private content, book clubs, internal sharing within communities, or a private

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.501
radio station for family.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:39.906
For instance, a dad who often tells his kids bedtime stories has to go on a business trip for a month.

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:43.409
While he's away, he can record episodes and push them to this private station

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:45.445
that's just for him and his kids.

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:49.248
I think that would be a really wonderful thing. However, this all sounds great,

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.150
but in practice, there are two pain points.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:55.421
First, making a podcast is a creative task, and most people won't do it proactively.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:59.859
Second, even if you use an AI tool like NotebookLM to generate the podcast,

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:03.329
the process of downloading it, uploading it to Google Drive, then logging into

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:05.231
Dear Tape to publish it...

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:07.133
that whole workflow is still very tedious.

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.802
So after using it for a while, even I stopped.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.505
But then, things took a turn. (Intro music)

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:13.005
You see,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:16.042
I finished making Dear Tape around July 2025,

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:16.943
using Replit,

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.945
an online Vibe Coding platform.

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:24.751
But later I started using local AI development tools on my computer, like Claude Code.

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:25.952
And then I realized something.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:26.719
And that is,

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:29.622
the hardest part is just installing it on your computer.

00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:30.289
Once it's installed,

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.859
it's like you've invited an AI onto your own machine.

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:33.960
It can not only write code,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.363
it can help you do everything you can do.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:38.831
You just need to tell it what you need,

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:39.899
and it will find the tools itself,

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.135
or even write its own tools to get the job done.

00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:44.604
And the recently emerged OpenClaw is a similar tool.

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:45.805
Except it's more interesting.

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:47.840
It's like an AI employee living on your computer,

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.778
but you can assign it tasks remotely through a chat app on your phone.

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:52.478
So I thought,

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:57.150
why not let the AI on my computer directly control NotebookLM to generate podcasts,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.919
and then automatically publish them to Dear Tape?

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:00.953
To achieve this,

00:07:00.953 --> 00:07:02.555
I needed three things.

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:02.955
First,

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:04.223
an AI that can control the computer,

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:08.294
like Claude Code or the more recent OpenClaw.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:10.830
Second, a tool for NotebookLM. On GitHub,

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.267
there's an open-source project called NotebookLM_PY

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.335
that allows an AI to automatically create notebooks,

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:16.836
upload materials,

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:18.671
and generate podcasts.

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:19.238
Third,

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:23.376
a way for the AI to directly control Dear Tape.

00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:24.444
I had the first two,

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:25.778
but what about the third one?

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:26.212
Uh,

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.014
if you've also gotten into Vibe Coding,

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.216
you definitely know the concept of an API.

00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:32.118
You can simply think of it as an interface

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:33.986
that lets a program directly call a service.

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:37.390
For example, the voice-to-text tool I made before, Flash Notes,

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:39.525
used OpenAI's API.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:43.763
So I thought, why don't I also make an API for Dear Tape,

00:07:43.763 --> 00:07:45.198
let the AI call it,

00:07:45.198 --> 00:07:47.400
and automatically publish podcasts?

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.571
So I had the AI help me add this feature.

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:54.373
Now, all three things I needed were ready.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:57.643
Now every Dear Tape user can get their own API.

00:07:57.643 --> 00:08:01.347
Just tell your OpenClaw or Claude Code about the method I mentioned,

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:04.550
and it can set up the entire automated workflow for you.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.920
And that brings us to the scene from the beginning of the video. Now for me,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:08.955
whenever I want to listen to a podcast,

00:08:08.955 --> 00:08:11.023
I just have to tell OpenClaw.

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:11.591
For example,

00:08:11.591 --> 00:08:12.892
uh, I'm reading a book recently.

00:08:12.892 --> 00:08:13.726
I can say,

00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:15.728
"Hey, turn this book into a podcast for me,"

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:17.597
and it will automatically handle everything:

00:08:17.597 --> 00:08:18.464
find the book,

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:21.167
split it into chapters, save them to my Obsidian notes,

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:23.369
upload it to NotebookLM to generate the podcast,

00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:26.005
and push it to Apple Podcasts via Dear Tape.

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:26.606
You know what?

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:30.610
When I connected this whole process of podcast generation and publishing,

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:31.711
and whenever I want to listen to something,

00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:32.979
I just have to say a single sentence,

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:37.083
and then I can hear a podcast made just for me on my phone...

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.784
that feeling is truly magical.

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:41.120
And it genuinely saves me a lot of time.

00:08:41.454 --> 00:08:42.788
So, that's my Dear Tape.

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:44.490
Everyone can start using it for free right now

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:45.458
to make your own private podcasts.

00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:51.197
And there are more features waiting to be discovered. I know that at this point, many of you want a detailed tutorial.

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:55.034
Actually, the easiest way is to just show the content of my video to your AI.

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:57.003
It will help you configure everything step-by-step.

00:08:57.003 --> 00:08:59.805
You can also join our new, free community space,

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:03.342
which is dedicated to sharing and discussing the use of OpenClaw and Dear Tape.

00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:04.810
The link is below the video.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:06.746
Of course, you can also build your own Dear Tape,

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:08.080
completely for your own use.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.482
That was unthinkable in the past,

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:10.616
but in the age of AI,

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:11.484
if I can do it,

00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:12.552
so can you.

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:14.887
Often, what stops us isn't the technology,

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:16.622
but our own mental blocks.

00:09:16.622 --> 00:09:16.989
In fact,

00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:18.858
Dear Tape isn't the only project I've made.

00:09:18.858 --> 00:09:19.659
Over the past few months,

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:24.096
I've built a lot of internal tools and some apps that are available for everyone to use.

00:09:24.096 --> 00:09:26.499
These apps are installed on my phone right now,

00:09:26.499 --> 00:09:28.668
and I genuinely use them every day.

00:09:28.668 --> 00:09:29.502
It's not a gimmick.

00:09:29.502 --> 00:09:30.136
So,

00:09:30.136 --> 00:09:32.438
I've decided to start a special series on this channel

00:09:32.438 --> 00:09:34.907
called "My AI Junk,"

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:36.442
to share the apps I've made

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:38.144
and give you all some inspiration.

00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:40.246
Dear Tape is the first episode of this series. Of course,

00:09:40.246 --> 00:09:41.681
besides sharing my "AI junk,"

00:09:41.681 --> 00:09:44.417
I also have many insights from learning about AI

00:09:44.417 --> 00:09:45.585
that I hope to share with you.

00:09:45.585 --> 00:09:47.887
So I've started a new channel

00:09:47.887 --> 00:09:49.689
called "Powered by Passion,"

00:09:49.689 --> 00:09:52.358
dedicated to discussing the technical details and thoughts on AI.

00:09:52.358 --> 00:09:53.893
I hope you'll follow it.

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:54.260
Of course,

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.629
if you want to learn AI from scratch and empower yourself,

00:09:56.629 --> 00:09:59.298
you're also welcome to join our "Powered by Passion" community.

00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:01.067
Besides the free section I just mentioned,

00:10:01.067 --> 00:10:02.768
we also have a large number of subscription courses

00:10:02.768 --> 00:10:05.037
covering all aspects of Vibe Coding,

00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:07.306
to help beginners like me get started from zero.

00:10:07.306 --> 00:10:11.310
We're even planning to launch a course that teaches you how to replicate Dear Tape.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.446
It will be a very interesting experience,

00:10:13.446 --> 00:10:16.682
because you won't just get a Dear Tape you can use yourself,

00:10:16.682 --> 00:10:20.686
more importantly, you'll learn to use AI by building a project hands-on,

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:21.587
bring your own ideas to life,

00:10:21.587 --> 00:10:24.423
and build momentum for your next breakthrough. OK,

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:26.258
that's all for this video.

00:10:26.258 --> 00:10:27.093
Thanks for watching.

00:10:27.093 --> 00:10:28.361
See you next time.

00:10:28.361 --> 00:10:30.863
Bye-bye.

